{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans","id":1475282,"title":"M20 Permanent effects on humans","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-02-22T19:18:43-08:00","id":1475282,"title":"M20 Permanent effects on humans","content":"Greetings,<br /> <br /> <br /> I am not very familiar with M20 Ascension so maybe the questions are badly stated, if so please correct me: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>What level of the Life (and others if needed) Sphere is required to permanently<b>* </b>remove physical conditions** such as poor eye sight, renal insufficiency, etc?</li> <li>What level of the Life (and others if needed) Sphere is required to permanently* gain the Merit <b>Unaging </b>or its effects?</li> </ol><br /> <br /> Thanks to all,<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>*By permanently I mean not subject to a dispelling effect.<br /> **Conditions will be almost anything that is a physical flaw, i.e. Impediment (this is the main one), Addiction, Permanent Wound, Aging perhaps.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-22T21:51:51-08:00","id":1475296,"title":"","content":"Traditionally in Mage, rewriting a pattern permanently (be it a life pattern, matter pattern, spirit or whatev) is simply a matter of 'more successes than usual!'.<br /> <br /> The M20 feats table (page 502) suggests 20 successes for that.<br /> In Revised it was more along the lines of 'Double the successes required', but in M20 you need less total successes in general unless you use the optional Revised-style split-successes rules for arete rolls, so 'double the required successes' like in Revised might be too low in many cases.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, that ~20 successes guideline for a truely permanent life-pattern rewrite explains why there are canonically Mages with physical limitations that never got fixed. It's probably quite easier for matter patterns.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, that said, something very important!<br /> Effects can only be made truely &quot;non-magically&quot; permanent (i.e. non-unweavable and detectable), if what they are doing would result in non-supernatural results in the end. Things that are accepted by normal reality as..normal.<br /> Fixed eyes? That's fine, working eyes are a 'normal' thing. (Ignoring the way of how they got fixed).<br /> Special and weird materials that, however, make chemically and physically sense? Sure. Watch the Mage's paradigm though, it needs to work along those lines. i.e. scientific paradigms work best, and still 'impossible 'materials probably can't be perma'd safely.<br /> <br /> Not aging? That can't be made permanent at all in a non-magical manner, unless the Mage's paradigm involves a very strict set of scientific viewpoints which relate to cell telomeres, and genetic setup that for example allows things like the RL lobsters to (supposedly) be effectively immortal.<br /> I'd say that until somehow this is actually brought to the Masses' normal reality through actual labwork (i.e. probably by Technocratic measures or Etherites somehow managing to push it to the mass media, and in a way that can be replicated through mundane means after that), it's not possible to perma-unmagic this effect due to unbelief and paradox. It will always stay an ongoing magical effect til then.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 02-23-2022, 12:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T05:32:02-08:00","id":1475308,"title":"","content":"There's a bit of play here. Growing and regenerating limbs is listed as an impressive feat, which is four successes. Permanently altering a pattern is 20 successes. Which one removing physical conditions falls under depends on how we interpret patterns. Does regrowing a lost limb also restore the pattern of the limb, or is the spell just bringing the body back to a state where it matches the pattern. Given that aggravated damage is considered to be actual pattern damage I'm inclined to say the former.<br /> <br /> If so then healing physical conditions, even those not caused by injury, is likely an Impressive Feat (4 successes). If not, healing conditions due to injury is Impressive while healing &quot;natural&quot; conditions is Legendary.<br /> <br /> It is unclear whether the regenerating limbs requires an additional 6+ successes to make it permanent, that probably depends on the needs of your table. &quot;Permanently rewriting a pattern&quot; should not require an extra 6 successes, though, else why would you call it permanent.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T05:55:26-08:00","id":1475312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475308#post1475308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Does regrowing a lost limb also restore the pattern of the limb, or is the spell just bringing the body back to a state where it matches the pattern. Given that aggravated damage is considered to be actual pattern damage I'm inclined to say the former.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aggravated damage is not necessarily pattern damage. It *can* be in the case of direct Life or Prime attacks, but mundane acid, while aggravated, does not touch the pattern instantly, just like a club doesn't.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, that said, I remember from Revised that fresh wounds, be it bruises or cut-off limbs, essentially is initially a mismatch between the physical side of things and the pattern.<br /> <br /> Over time, the pattern adjusts to match the new status quo - pretty much probably going hand-in-hand with the natural healing process. So, when you've got scars, a naturally healed stump etc, then fixing that would be a pattern alteration. Regrowing a recently cut-off limb, closing wounds etc. would be simple healing, as you shift things back to the still unchanged pattern.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T07:48:04-08:00","id":1475319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475312#post1475312\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Aggravated damage is not necessarily pattern damage. It *can* be in the case of direct Life or Prime attacks, but mundane acid, while aggravated, does not touch the pattern instantly, just like a club doesn't.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>The description of Aggravated damage seems to vary from book to book, and in M20 from section to section. M20, pg 336 indicates that pattern damage is one form of Aggravated damage. Pg 408 breaks the other way and indicates Agg damage is always pattern damage. I tend to go with the second as it gives a metaphysical distinction between Agg and Lethal, and because it explains why Quintessence is necessary to heal Aggravated damage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Now, that said, I remember from Revised that fresh wounds, be it bruises or cut-off limbs, essentially is initially a mismatch between the physical side of things and the pattern.<br /> <br /> Over time, the pattern adjusts to match the new status quo - pretty much probably going hand-in-hand with the natural healing process. So, when you've got scars, a naturally healed stump etc, then fixing that would be a pattern alteration. Regrowing a recently cut-off limb, closing wounds etc. would be simple healing, as you shift things back to the still unchanged pattern. </div> </div> </div>That's another way of resolving it. In some ways it makes sense that a long lost limb should be harder to restore than a fresh one. On the other hand it also makes sense that a long lost limb should be easier to restore than growing an extra limb. I suppose that might be more a paradigmatic consideration than a systems one, though.<br /> <br /> More notably, that's a really good argument for why replacing a lost limb permanently <i>isn't</i> a Legendary feat. If the loss of a limb permanently changes a pattern over time, the regain of one should have the same effect. Some mismatches between Pattern and form correct over time. At the very least that is a rational way of adjudicating it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T08:33:08-08:00","id":1475322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475296#post1475296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Traditionally in Mage, rewriting a pattern permanently (be it a life pattern, matter pattern, spirit or whatev) is simply a matter of 'more successes than usual!'.<br /> <br /> The M20 feats table (page 502) suggests 20 successes for that.<br /> In Revised it was more along the lines of 'Double the successes required', but in M20 you need less total successes in general unless you use the optional Revised-style split-successes rules for arete rolls, so 'double the required successes' like in Revised might be too low in many cases.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, that ~20 successes guideline for a truely permanent life-pattern rewrite explains why there are canonically Mages with physical limitations that never got fixed. It's probably quite easier for matter patterns.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, that said, something very important!<br /> Effects can only be made truely &quot;non-magically&quot; permanent (i.e. non-unweavable and detectable), if what they are doing would result in non-supernatural results in the end. Things that are accepted by normal reality as..normal.<br /> Fixed eyes? That's fine, working eyes are a 'normal' thing. (Ignoring the way of how they got fixed).<br /> Special and weird materials that, however, make chemically and physically sense? Sure. Watch the Mage's paradigm though, it needs to work along those lines. i.e. scientific paradigms work best, and still 'impossible 'materials probably can't be perma'd safely.<br /> <br /> Not aging? That can't be made permanent at all in a non-magical manner, unless the Mage's paradigm involves a very strict set of scientific viewpoints which relate to cell telomeres, and genetic setup that for example allows things like the RL lobsters to (supposedly) be effectively immortal.<br /> I'd say that until somehow this is actually brought to the Masses' normal reality through actual labwork (i.e. probably by Technocratic measures or Etherites somehow managing to push it to the mass media, and in a way that can be replicated through mundane means after that), it's not possible to perma-unmagic this effect due to unbelief and paradox. It will always stay an ongoing magical effect til then.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Thanks for the answer. Going a bit into details... Would the removal of conditions (question 1 on my post) be a matter of Life 2 + Several successes?<br /> <br /> And same about question 2, will it be a matter of Life 2, Life 3? (Plus a large number of successes?)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T15:50:43-08:00","id":1475368,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475322#post1475322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Thanks for the answer. Going a bit into details... Would the removal of conditions (question 1 on my post) be a matter of Life 2 + Several successes?<br /> <br /> And same about question 2, will it be a matter of Life 2, Life 3? (Plus a large number of successes?)</div> </div> </div> </div>I would think it\u2019s Life 2 for a mage to fix themself, and Life 3 to fix someone else.<br /> <br /> To gain unaging through Life magick probably isn\u2019t possible IMO. You could repair the damage from aging with life 3 (self) or life 4 (other), but I\u2019d rule it can never be permanent under normal circumstances and would require regular renewals that would increase in frequency the older the individual gets. Dunno that\u2019s just my thinking on the subject.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T17:08:16-08:00","id":1475377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>breggie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475368#post1475368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would think it\u2019s Life 2 for a mage to fix themself, and Life 3 to fix someone else.<br /> <br /> To gain unaging through Life magick probably isn\u2019t possible IMO. You could repair the damage from aging with life 3 (self) or life 4 (other), but I\u2019d rule it can never be permanent under normal circumstances and would require regular renewals that would increase in frequency the older the individual gets. Dunno that\u2019s just my thinking on the subject.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the reply.<br /> <br /> But what about the Unaging merit? If you can gain benefits equivalent to removing flaws, certainly is conceivable to gain merits, including Unaging (cheap at 2 dots).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T18:04:14-08:00","id":1475382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475377#post1475377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks for the reply.<br /> <br /> But what about the Unaging merit? If you can gain benefits equivalent to removing flaws, certainly is conceivable to gain merits, including Unaging (cheap at 2 dots).</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019ve never seen an ST, including myself, that allows buying merits in game. I\u2019d rule no.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26904-breggie","name":"breggie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26904_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-23T21:44:41-08:00","id":1475392,"title":"","content":"You need to watch for &quot;Pattern Bleed.&quot; Making an obviously unnatural change in a pattern permanent can require regular ingestion of Quintessence. And you court paradox. <br /> <br /> However, if the change is subtle and hard to notice, you've got more leeway. Say a man had testicular carsanoma when young, and lost one testicle and his lymph nodes in his chest and abdomen. Replacing what was lost, especially as it's all hidden from public view, would be infinitely less trouble than say functional wings. The less visible any change is the safer. A mage of average looks, who was never famous, risks far less paradox in extending their life through magick than a famously good looking Hollywood Star who is now a mage. The more eyeballs an enchantment attracts, the higher the risk of paradox.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-02T18:14:01-08:00","id":1475693,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>lbeaumanior</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475377#post1475377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the reply.<br /> <br /> But what about the Unaging merit? If you can gain benefits equivalent to removing flaws, certainly is conceivable to gain merits, including Unaging (cheap at 2 dots).</div> </div> </div> </div>Merits and Flaws in M:tA already consider the fact that Magic exists, that means two things:<br /> <br /> A) Merits frequently circumvent some of the hardest limits of Magic, usually by ignoring Paradox, Pattern Bleeding, and similar effects;<br /> <br /> B) Flaws frequently state directly the rules for how they operate when someone tries to overcome them with Magic, so just magicking them away isn't a thing;<br /> <br /> C) There are rules for dealing with any effect on a PC that gives something worth XP. When A) and B) don't apply, adapt and use those.<br /> <br /> Specifically on the matter of <b>Unaging</b>, there's a rule in Revised IIRC that states that effects to stave aging away start to accrue Permadox at 100 years, not to say that they may come to attract unweaving and regular Paradox far before when meaningfully witnessed. This Permadox starts at 1 and increases every 50 years.<br /> <br /> I recommend following this ruling or something similar if you want to stay close to the canon lore, because the change in Consensus that started it, making permanent immortality impossible, is well established and had several consequences through the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T05:17:17-08:00","id":1475891,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475693#post1475693\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Specifically on the matter of <b>Unaging</b>, there's a rule in Revised IIRC that states that effects to stave aging away start to accrue Permadox at 100 years, not to say that they may come to attract unweaving and regular Paradox far before when meaningfully witnessed. This Permadox starts at 1 and increases every 50 years.<br /> <br /> I recommend following this ruling or something similar if you want to stay close to the canon lore, because the change in Consensus that started it, making permanent immortality impossible, is well established and had several consequences through the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>On the other hand, if you hate Revised crap and love the idea of powerful mages being immortal by default (unless for some reason they can't or won't to be) as much as I do, you can drop that Revised rule in the dustbin it deserves with the rest of that accursed edition, and solve the canon lore issues by assuming that Tremere was an idiot that got mistaken about his predictions of the future. The Order of Reason's rise was indeed coming (although it was partially a case of self-fulfilling prophecy since without the Tremere's treachery, the Craftmasons in all likelihood would have got crushed) to change the Consensus, but the change would not make permanent immortality any more difficult or troublesome that other cases of powerful Vulgar magic. Anything Tremere wrought was a tragic mistake, overreaction, and self-inflicted harm that doomed Tradition mages and the world to a millennium of Technocracy tyranny. Let Revised stupid stay obsolete and irrelevant. As far as I am concerned, nothing happened between 2nd Ediition and M20, and I heartily suggest anyone but hardcore Revised fans to take the same stance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-06-2022, 08:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T06:12:59-08:00","id":1475893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475891#post1475891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> On the other hand, if you hate Revised crap and love the idea of powerful mages being immortal by default (unless for some reason they can't or won't to be) as much as I do, you can drop that Revised rule in the dustbin it deserves with the rest of that accursed edition, and solve the canon lore issues by assuming that Tremere was an idiot that got mistaken about his predictions of the future. The Order of Reason's rise was indeed coming (although it was partially a case of self-fulfilling prophecy since without the Tremere's treachery, the Craftmasons in all likelihood would have got crushed) to change the Consensus, but the change would not make permanent immortality any more difficult or troublesome that other cases of powerful Vulgar magic. Anything Tremere wrought was a tragic mistake, overreaction, and self-inflicted harm that doomed Tradition mages and the world to a millennium of Technocracy tyranny. Let Revised stupid stay obsolete and irrelevant. As far as I am concerned, nothing happened between 2nd Ediition and M20, and I heartily suggest anyone but hardcore Revised fans to take the same stance.</div> </div> </div> </div>I once ran a post-apocalyptic campaign were almost all Mages aged more slowly than than Sleepers. I simply took the square of the Mage's Arete and divided their aging rate by that. Thus a Mage with an Arete of three aged at one-ninth","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T06:20:21-08:00","id":1475894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475891#post1475891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> On the other hand, if you hate Revised crap and love the idea of powerful mages being immortal by default (unless for some reason they can't or won't to be) as much as I do, you can drop that Revised rule in the dustbin it deserves with the rest of that accursed edition, and solve the canon lore issues by assuming that Tremere was an idiot that got mistaken about his predictions of the future. The Order of Reason's rise was indeed coming (although it was partially a case of self-fulfilling prophecy since without the Tremere's treachery, the Craftmasons in all likelihood would have got crushed) to change the Consensus, but the change would not make permanent immortality any more difficult or troublesome that other cases of powerful Vulgar magic. Anything Tremere wrought was a tragic mistake, overreaction, and self-inflicted harm that doomed Tradition mages and the world to a millennium of Technocracy tyranny. Let Revised stupid stay obsolete and irrelevant. As far as I am concerned, nothing happened between 2nd Ediition and M20, and I heartily suggest anyone but hardcore Revised fans to take the same stance.</div> </div> </div> </div>I once ran a post-apocalyptic campaign were almost all Mages aged more slowly than than Sleepers. I simply took the square of the Mage's Arete and divided their aging rate by that. Thus a Mage with an Arete of three aged at one-ninth of the normal rate. Mages did have problems in this setting, mainly paradox, which was weaker than in the normal game, caused Mages to slowly accumulate Arcane until they could no longer interact with Sleepers. <br /> <br /> Still, your game your rules. I remember one online setting for M:tA were paradox was sidelined by the Traditions. However, most of the Mages fell to hubris and oppressed the Sleepers. <br /> <br /> I another online homebrew setting were dressing as a Wizard and using foci like wands and other dramatic tools reduced paradox dramatically. Thus the Technocracy could spot you easily but you could fight back hard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 03-06-2022, 12:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T09:13:49-08:00","id":1475903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475891#post1475891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the other hand, if you hate Revised crap and love the idea of powerful mages being immortal by default (unless for some reason they can't or won't to be) as much as I do, you can drop that Revised rule in the dustbin it deserves with the rest of that accursed edition, and solve the canon lore issues by assuming that Tremere was an idiot that got mistaken about his predictions of the future. The Order of Reason's rise was indeed coming (although it was partially a case of self-fulfilling prophecy since without the Tremere's treachery, the Craftmasons in all likelihood would have got crushed) to change the Consensus, but the change would not make permanent immortality any more difficult or troublesome that other cases of powerful Vulgar magic. Anything Tremere wrought was a tragic mistake, overreaction, and self-inflicted harm that doomed Tradition mages and the world to a millennium of Technocracy tyranny. Let Revised stupid stay obsolete and irrelevant. As far as I am concerned, nothing happened between 2nd Ediition and M20, and I heartily suggest anyone but hardcore Revised fans to take the same stance.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I do not agree with many of Revised stances on making magic harder, this level of vitriol is undeserved.<br /> <br /> The setting has had the idea that too powerful magic and immortal Mages duel in the Umbra for a long time and it didn't change. Any rule about Paradox, Permadox and Consensus can be ignored in the Umbra, a fact 2nd edition used a lot.<br /> <br /> Indeed anyone that prefers immortal mages as default (I don't, by the way, I think it boring and problematic) can do so freely, but since the OP question is about the actual rules of the game, defending the thorough disconnect with said rules is kind of pointless. For the record, though, it would hardly be a problem from a balance standpoint, unless in extremely specific circumstances that are unlikely to arise in the great majority of games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-06T14:14:51-08:00","id":1475940,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1475282-m20-permanent-effects-on-humans?p=1475891#post1475891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>On the other hand, if you hate Revised crap and love the idea of powerful mages being immortal by default (unless for some reason they can't or won't to be) as much as I do, you can drop that Revised rule in the dustbin it deserves with the rest of that accursed edition, and solve the canon lore issues by assuming that Tremere was an idiot that got mistaken about his predictions of the future. </b>The Order of Reason's rise was indeed coming (although it was partially a case of self-fulfilling prophecy since without the Tremere's treachery, the Craftmasons in all likelihood would have got crushed) to change the Consensus, but the change would not make permanent immortality any more difficult or troublesome that other cases of powerful Vulgar magic. Anything Tremere wrought was a tragic mistake, overreaction, and self-inflicted harm that doomed Tradition mages and the world to a millennium of Technocracy tyranny. Let Revised stupid stay obsolete and irrelevant. As far as I am concerned, nothing happened between 2nd Ediition and M20, and I heartily suggest anyone but hardcore Revised fans to take the same stance.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b>No Edition Warring. Consider this a warning.</b></span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=